## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-context saga design with event sourcing, CQRS, and tenancy. It probes how compensation flows across multiple bounded contexts and how to ensure correctness under retries.

## Key Concepts

- Saga orchestration vs choreography
- Event-sourced cross-tenant aggregates
- Idempotent handlers using (tenantId, orderId)
- Compensation patterns: revert inventory, refund, notify

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal orchestrator snippet
class Orchestrator {
  handle(event) {
    switch(event.type) {
      case 'InventoryReserved':
        publish({ type:'PaymentCapture', orderId: event.orderId, tenantId: event.tenantId});
        break;
      case 'ShippingFailed':
        publish({ type:'Compensate', orderId: event.orderId, tenantId: event.tenantId, reason:'Shipping failed' });
        break;
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotent handlers across tenants?
- How do you evolve event schemas without breaking consumers?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of basic sharding and replication in a realistic chat system. Emphasizes data locality, tenant isolation, and safe rebalancing with minimal downtime.

## Key Concepts

- Shard key choice: user_id for even distribution
- Replication factor: 3 for durability
- Hot-partition handling: detect and migrate
- Rebalancing: rolling data moves
- Read path with tenant checks: enforce isolation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo JS shard map
class ShardMap {
  constructor(size) {
    this.size = size;
    this.map = new Map();
  }
  getShard(userId) {
    const idx = userId % this.size;
    return this.map.get(idx) || idx;
  }
}
```

